WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.330
Whether you’re a soap artisan starting an
etsy store, a chef opening a food truck, the

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:09.970
CEO of a automobile company, or a freelance
graphic designer, one of the most critical

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:15.000
business decisions you’ll have to make is
what price to ask for your goods or services.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.130
Charge too much and you lose customers, but
charge too little and you lose money.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:24.300
You might remember this graph from your high
school Economics class. It suggests that as

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.300
the price of a product increases, the supply
(the number of people who want to sell it)

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:34.699
goes up, but the demand (the number of people
who want to buy it) goes down. Supposedly

00:00:34.699 --> 00:00:40.190
there’s a magic number where these two lines
meet that’s high enough to keep sellers

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:42.839
sellin’ but low enough to keep buyers buyin’.

00:00:42.839 --> 00:00:47.920
It’s called the equilibrium price, and it’s
a useful theoretical concept, but it’s not

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:53.100
much help to Ruth here, who’s about to launch
a line of homemade bath soaps on Etsy. Even

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.569
with a lot of expensive market research, it
would be very difficult for her to predict

00:00:57.569 --> 00:01:01.940
how much people would be willing to pay for
her Lavender Lemongrass Facial Scrub.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:07.170
She can, however, calculate her costs--in
other words, how much time and money she has

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:12.549
to spend to make one bar. Assuming that
her costs stay the same, the more she charges

00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:18.299
above that, the more her profit margin will
increase... but the more her sales will decrease.

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:23.140
That is, if this graph is an accurate representation
of how customers behave.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:28.229
But this graph doesn’t take into account
one crucial fact: that humans aren’t robots,

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:32.320
and our financial behavior doesn’t track
neatly along a straight line.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.500
Just ask Louis Vuitton if raising prices always
lowers demand.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.600
In 2007, a group of researchers tried to get
into customers’ heads… literally. Using

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.740
an MRI machine, they found that just looking
at prices activated the areas of the brain

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:01.450
associated with physical pain. Funny enough,
that response was less when they took away

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:07.060
the dollar sign, even less without the decimals,
and least of all when written out. Now you

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:10.380
know how fancy restaurants get away with $40
dollar chicken.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:16.530
That painful feeling of losing something (like
money) is known in economics as negative utility.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:22.050
Conversely, positive utility is the satisfaction
we get from gaining or consuming something.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:27.269
And that doesn’t necessarily mean a physical
benefit. The sense of status one gets from

00:02:27.269 --> 00:02:33.370
buying a $5,000 designer handbag is positive
utility. So is the peace of mind of having

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:38.939
a $20 fire extinguisher under your sink even
if you never use it. It’s all about emotion.

00:02:38.939 --> 00:02:44.120
Every customer wants to walk away from every
transaction with net positive utility, meaning

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.980
they want to feel like they gained more than
they lost. But how do customers know whether

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:55.099
something is over- or under-priced? Short
answer: they don’t. Not really. I mean,

00:02:55.099 --> 00:03:01.880
do you know how much it costs to manufacture
a computer? Or a candy bar? Without that information,

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:06.970
consumers are forced to guess at a product’s
value, and they do that through comparisons.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:12.220
In study after study, when presented with
multiple brands at varying price-points, most

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:17.860
consumers tend to gravitate towards the median
price. That is, they assume the most expensive

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:24.160
option is probably over-priced, but they’re
also wary that the cheapest might be an inferior

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.830
product, so the the one right in the middle
is their best estimation of the product’s

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:29.830
true value.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.990
This is sometimes referred to as the “magic
of the middle,” and understanding it is

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:38.640
essential to attracting customers. Sarah is
a freelance graphic designer and she’s asked

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.390
to bid on a job she really wants. But she’s
relatively new to the industry and not sure

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:47.209
how much she should quote. She asks other
professionals in the field what they typically

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:53.209
charge, and even cold calls some design companies
pretending to be a client--not very honest,

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:58.879
but revealing--and finds out that graphic
design rates in her area run from $50-150

00:03:58.879 --> 00:04:00.090
dollars per hour.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:05.010
Sarah really wants this job, so she might
be tempted to offer a rock-bottom rate of

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:10.569
$25 an hour, but to many potential clients,
that could be a red flag. They might assume

00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:16.670
that someone offering substandard prices will
deliver substandard work. She decides to quote

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:22.660
just under the median price: $80 an hour,
and thanks to her strong portfolio, she lands

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:23.440
the job!

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.340
In other words, price is often interpreted
as an indicator of quality. This should be

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:32.950
very relevant to someone like Ruth, whose
customers will only buy her fancy bath soaps

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:37.940
if they believe they’re fancy. Her potential
buyers might enjoy saving money on socks or

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:42.670
toilet paper, but they’re not looking to
skimp on Mother’s Day gifts. How powerful

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:48.320
is the effect of price on psychology? One
medical study found that placebos were more

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.760
effective if the patients receiving them believed
they cost more.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:58.720
The fact is that it’s very difficult to
make low prices a successful business strategy.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.560
Partly because they are often interpreted
as an indicator of poor quality, but it’s

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:09.440
also because average profit margins are much
smaller than people assume. A survey found

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:16.100
that American consumers believed the average
profit margin in the U.S. to be 46%. That

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:22.320
is, they assumed that for every dollar a company
takes in, it will keep 46 cents in profit.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.330
The actual number? Closer to 5%.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:30.200
To understand how sensitive profit margins
are to pricing, take the theoretical example

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:35.160
of Martin, who owns and operates a couple
food trucks selling Cuban sandwiches. Martin

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:43.100
sells about 100 sandwiches a day at $8 a sandwich,
with a total yearly revenue of around $300,000.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:48.290
After subtracting all of his food, labor and
operating costs, he’s left with about $45,000

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:53.510
in profits a year, for a respectable profit
margin of 15%.

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:58.760
But Martin wonders if he could attract more
business by lowering his prices. After all,

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:04.430
the pizza place next door sells two slices
for $7… If he knocked just 75 cents off

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:08.990
the sandwich price, maybe he could poach some
of their customers. He does the math and is

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:14.050
shocked to discover that even at that modest
price decrease, his yearly profits would be

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:20.310
slashed to under $20,000. He’d have to sell
more than twice as many sandwiches as he

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:25.180
is now… just to break even. And how many
more customers could he realistically expect

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:27.840
to gain from such a small discount?

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.080
This can be even more disastrous for an entrepreneur
like Gabe, who sells men’s razors online.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:38.090
If he offers a steep discount for a limited
time, he could indeed see a jump in sales…

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:43.240
but those might just be his regular customers
taking advantage of the sale to stockpile--people

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.480
who would’ve eventually bought the razors
anyway at the regular price.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:52.060
This is what happened to General Motors in
2005, when they announced an unprecedented

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:57.390
price cut in their cars. Sales skyrocketed
for a couple months, and then plummeted. Essentially,

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:02.190
they were poaching customers from future quarters.
They ended up losing billions.

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:05.790
If you want to try to beat the competition
with low prices, you’ll have to make it

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:10.700
a part of your business plan from the get-go.
That means scrupulously keeping costs and

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:15.180
wages as low as possible, while constantly
communicating to your customers that your

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.610
product is reliable.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:21.690
Or, you could focus instead on just making
a great product, ask a price that you genuinely

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:25.660
think it’s worth, and be ready to adjust
to feedback from your market.

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:26.820
And that’s our two cents!

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:32.200
If you’d like to dig deeper into the thrilling
world of prices, you can check out “Confessions

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.740
of the Pricing Man” by Hermann Simon.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.340
If you've ever sold anything or started a small business, how did you decide on a price?

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:42.080
Let us know in the comments.

